  "method": "Centre fixed by two independent least-squares circle fits to OpenStreetMap geometry that agree to within 0.2 m: (a) OSM way 21825283 'Booster Ring Road' (closed loop, 25 vertices, fit centre 41.836555,-88.263493, fitted radius 58.7 m, rms 0.8 m; path length 367.9 m, consistent with this road being an access loop inside the ring, not the ring itself); (b) OSM ways 272646259 'Booster Tower West' and 272646261 'Booster Tower East' (92 vertices of two arc-shaped RF-gallery building footprints that hug the ring wall, fit centre 41.836557,-88.263493, fitted radius 69.5 m, rms 7.9 m). Both centres coincide to <1 m. The Booster is sourced as a '474-meter-circumference synchrotron' (src:fermilab-miniboone-flux-paper-2009); this is treated as an implicit circularity statement together with the two independent near-circular OSM tracings (ring-road rms 0.8 m over a 59 m radius, i.e. <2% relative deviation). Radius is set from the sourced circumference (474/(2*pi) = 75.44 m) rather than either fitted OSM radius, since both OSM tracings are physically inside/at the wall of the true beam ring, consistently smaller than the accelerator's own radius by 6-17 m. Haversine on R=6371008.8 m (schema.great_circle_m). Release pass (gaps stream, 2026-10-25): the first point of the 8 GeV line in the Main Injector TDR survey (the Booster extraction point), converted with the Fermilab Site Coordinate System, lies 76.3 m from this centre against the 75.44 m radius (1.1 %), an independent confirmation from published survey data; accuracy_m lowered from 30 to 10.",
  "name": "Fermilab Booster ring (derived circle)",
